## Why This Is Asked

Tests system design for real-time collaboration at scale, including choice of transport, state partitioning, conflict resolution, and delivery guarantees.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time collaboration data models (CRDTs vs OT)
- Transport choices (WebSocket vs gRPC)
- Per-room sharding and snapshotting
- Exactly-once vs at-least-once semantics

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple CRDT merge sketch
class ORSet {
  constructor() { this.add = new Set(); this.remove = new Set(); }
  apply(op) { if (op.type === 'add') this.add.add(op.id); else if (op.type === 'remove') this.remove.add(op.id); }
  value() { return [...this.add].filter(id => !this.remove.has(id)); }
  merge(other) { this.add = new Set([...this.add, ...other.add]); this.remove = new Set([...this.remove, ...other.remove]); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you horizontally scale 10k rooms with 200 concurrent editors?
- How do you ensure client reconnection replays without duplication?

## Why This Is Asked
The tokenized explanation approach isolates training data exposure, supporting privacy and auditability for beginners.

## Key Concepts
- JWT-based explanation tokens, HS256 signing
- model_versioning, token rotation, expiry
- input validation and per-user audit

## Code Example
```javascript
// Issue explain token (Node.js)
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');
function issueExplainToken(user_id, model_version, expiryMs, secret) {
  const payload = { sub: user_id, mv: model_version, exp: Math.floor(Date.now()/1000) + expiryMs/1000 };
  return jwt.sign(payload, secret, { algorithm: 'HS256', audience: user_id });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate signing keys and invalidate tokens after a model version update?
- How would you test for token forgery or replay attacks?

